<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Jetavanaramaya</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">3rd century</span> · <a href="/centuries/3rd-century">3rd century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Jetavanaramaya is a Buddhist stupa in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anuradhapura">Anuradhapura</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sri-lanka">Sri Lanka</a>, begun by King Mahasena in the 3rd century CE. Built of brick and originally about 122 metres high, it was among the tallest structures of the ancient world. It belonged to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jetavana">Jetavana</a> monastery, which rivalled the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahavihara">Mahavihara</a> and Abhayagiri as a centre of Buddhist learning.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/jetavanaramaya.jpg" alt="Jetavanaramaya" width="320" height="234" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Wimukthi Bandara · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Jetavanaramaya_Stupa_profile.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
